 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1790). Words follow the Peters engraving's spelling and punctuation: Si mora si, oh Dei, nè, o il labbro, Don Alfonso's “in pocchi istanti” (the libretto has pochi; IPA ˈpɔ.ki) and the officers' “il tristo effetto” (the libretto has triste; tristo is the older form of the same word, and its IPA follows the print, ˈtri.sto). The sisters' “me” in bar 1 ends the previous piece and is not in a phrase here. Ferrando and Guglielmo sing the same words, and all five sing “Ah che del sole il raggio” from bar 51; each singer has his or her own phrases. IPA, word meanings and translations come from the app's reviewed Così libretto drafts (L37, IPA checked against Castel), repeated where the score repeats the text; repeated fragments have short literal translations. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
